WEBVTT

preparation-01
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Wash your hands and don PPE.

preparation-02
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Prepare the Arclight: select the brightest light setting, push both lens racks fully up, and use a quiet, dim room.

preparation-03
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:31.000
Bring the sight hole close to your eye. Don't shine the light into your own eye, or hold it sideways.

preparation-04
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:48.000
A newborn should be securely swaddled. Ask a parent to hold an older baby, and a child who can sit steadily may sit on their own.

examination-01
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.000
Start at about arm's length.

examination-02
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Move from side to side a little, and get closer if you need a more detailed view.

examination-03
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:20.000
At about arm's length, encourage the child to look towards you. Examine both eyes at the same time. The appearance will vary depending on how far away you are and where the child is looking.

examination-04
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:32.000
There should be no difference in the brightness or colour of the two fundal reflexes. Any difference in colour or brightness, or absence of a reflex, is abnormal. These cases need to be referred.

examination-05
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:47.000
Normal reflex colour varies with retinal pigmentation. It may appear yellow-white or bluish, orange-red, or orange-yellow. These are all healthy and normal appearances for these children.

eyes-open-01
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Ask the parent to hold the newborn securely, swaddled with the arms tucked in.

eyes-open-02
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:13.000
If the eyes are open, try observing the reflexes in both eyes at the same time, without touching the baby.

eyes-closed-02
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:35.000
If the baby is asleep, try not to wake them. Gently open and examine one eye at a time. A calm, gentle, and patient approach is important.

possible-findings-01
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Brief, occasional squints are common in the first month and usually disappear by three months. If they persist after three months, refer the child.

unclear-findings-01
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:57.000
If the finding is unclear, follow the next three steps.

unclear-findings-02
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:07.000
First, compare the baby's reflex with the parent's. They should look similar.

unclear-findings-03
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:14.000
Second, if you are still unsure, ask a colleague for a second opinion.

unclear-findings-04
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Third, if no colleague is available, obtain consent,

unclear-findings-05
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:37.000
attach the Arclight to the phone camera, record the reflex, and share the video securely for a second opinion.

possible-findings-02
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:45.000
Similar colour and brightness in both eyes are reassuring.

possible-findings-03
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:55.000
A colour difference, or partial or complete loss of a reflex, is abnormal and needs further assessment.

after-examination-01
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.000
Thank the parent and explain the findings and next steps.

after-examination-02
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:04.900
Repeat hand wash. This is the end of the fundal reflex examination.
